# Documentation sync (required)

When you change user-facing behaviour, architecture, storage, prefs, APIs, or install/setup flows, update **all** relevant docs in the same change — do not leave docs for a follow-up.

| Audience | Where |
| --- | --- |
| End users (how-to, concepts, tutorials, landing) | [`website/src/content/docs/`](website/src/content/docs/) (and landing [`index.mdx`](website/src/content/docs/index.mdx) if the feature set or pitch changes) |
| GitHub visitors (install, overview, API surface pointers) | [`README.md`](README.md) |
| Contributors / agents (architecture, storage, build) | [`doc/TECHNICAL.md`](doc/TECHNICAL.md) |

## What counts as a docs-worthy change

- New or renamed views, prefs, menus, or workflows
- Behaviour users would notice (pinning, import, publish, Gallery, Home, etc.)
- Storage formats, tags, note titles, collection names, or merge rules
- Public plugin API (`Zotero.Syllabus.api`) or developer setup commands
- Install, Zotero version requirements, or Connector/import platforms

## What to skip

- Pure refactors with no behaviour or contract change
- Typo/lint-only edits
- Internal-only test or CI churn that does not change how the product works

## How to update

1. **Website** — edit the matching Diátaxis page(s); add a page and sidebar entry in [`website/astro.config.mjs`](website/astro.config.mjs) if it is a new surface. Keep copy user-facing (not a dump of TECHNICAL.md).
2. **README.md** — keep the short overview accurate (install, surface list, links to the docs site and API). Do not re-expand the full user manual into the README.
3. **TECHNICAL.md** — document storage, sync, architecture, and contributor commands. Link out to the website for end-user steps instead of duplicating them.

If a change is docs-only on one surface, still check the other two for stale claims.
